- **micro_belief_sequence:** Belief 1 (B-106) Plenty of people lose money on a first store — I'm not the only one → Belief 2 (B-101) Most of those stores tried one product, or one batch, and closed when it didn't sell → Belief 3 (B-105) Fixing the store or buying more clicks for that same product didn't change it → Belief 4 (B-107) Sellers who do find a product that sells usually tested five to ten first, each on a small budget → Belief 5 (B-102 broken) So my store didn't show it can't work for me; it showed one guess missed → mechanism (M-1): the first store got one shot → **solution requirement** → product: Readymerce's staged release, each product tested on a budget he approves, an early review, what doesn't sell replaced (PT-04, F-05, F-07) — in a store in his name (F-11).
- **solution_requirement (he can predict it before the product name):** "The second try has to be several small tries, not one big one — products put up in turns, each on a budget I approve and can stop, with someone reading the results early and swapping what isn't selling, in a store that stays in my name."
- **biggest_leap:** BIGGEST LEAP: that Readymerce's $500 package actually runs enough stages and product tests to count as "several tries" — PT-19 per-stage product count UNKNOWN; PT-05 excludes ad spend, so the tries cost him extra ad money; 10 product_would_need (products per stage, default test budget + stop rule, who funds ad spend). Second, smaller leap: Belief 4 rests on a practitioner guide (Product Lair 2024), not a study.
